{"chunk_id": "chunk_c4ed3d85115d967cff340bbf", "paper_id": "arxiv_2002.05202", "arxiv_id": "2002.05202", "paper_title": "GLU Variants Improve Transformer", "version": "v1", "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.05202v1", "source_pdf": "data/corpus_80/pdfs/arxiv_2002.05202v1.pdf", "source_pdf_sha256": "7398ae8b163722a6b5410fb975722adbe6b5f6f9c7f1efa584f1c6b79eaa93e2", "section_id": "arxiv_2002.05202_v1_s0001", "section_title": "Abstract", "section_path": ["Abstract"], "section_kind": "body", "section_chunk_index": 0, "char_start": 0, "char_end": 499, "offset_scope": "normalized_section_text", "text": "Gated Linear Units [Dauphin et al., 2016] consist of the component-wise product of two linear pro- jections, one of which is first passed through a sigmoid function. Variations on GLU are possible, using different nonlinear (or even linear) functions in place of sigmoid. We test these variants in the feed- forward sublayers of the Transformer [Vaswani et al., 2017] sequence-to-sequence model, and find that some of them yield quality improvements over the typically-used ReLU or GELU activations.", "token_count": 134, "tokenizer": "BAAI/bge-m3", "page_numbers": [1], "source_spans": [{"paragraph_id": "p_111f478a253eb1995717", "page": 1, "bbox": [96.96, 250.08, 516.7, 302.84], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 499, "section_char_start": 0, "section_char_end": 499}], "parser_quality_flags": [], "chunker_version": "recursive-spans-v1", "config_id": "2361cccdddd6", "previous_chunk_id": null, "next_chunk_id": null}
{"chunk_id": "chunk_5bc00835212c44f134ff8162", "paper_id": "arxiv_2002.05202", "arxiv_id": "2002.05202", "paper_title": "GLU Variants Improve Transformer", "version": "v1", "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.05202v1", "source_pdf": "data/corpus_80/pdfs/arxiv_2002.05202v1.pdf", "source_pdf_sha256": "7398ae8b163722a6b5410fb975722adbe6b5f6f9c7f1efa584f1c6b79eaa93e2", "section_id": "arxiv_2002.05202_v1_s0002", "section_title": "1 Introduction", "section_path": ["1 Introduction"], "section_kind": "body", "section_chunk_index": 0, "char_start": 0, "char_end": 987, "offset_scope": "normalized_section_text", "text": "The Transformer [Vaswani et al., 2017] sequence-to-sequence model alternates between multi-head attention, and what it calls \"position-wise feed-forward networks\" (FFN). The FFN takes a vector x (the hidden repre- sentation at a particular position in the sequence) and passes it through two learned linear transformations, (represented by the matrices W1 and W2 and bias vectors b1 and b2). A rectified-linear (ReLU) [Glorot et al.,\n\n2011] activation function applied between the two linear transformations.\n\nFFN(x, W1, W2, b1, b2) = max(0, xW1 + b1)W2 + b2 (1)\n\nFollowing the T5 codebase [Raffel et al., 2019] 1, we use a version with no bias:\n\nFFNReLU(x, W1, W2) = max(xW1, 0)W2 (2)\n\nSubsequent work has proposed replacing the ReLU with other nonlinear activation functions such as Gaussian Error Linear Units, GELU(x) = xΦ(x) [Hendrycks and Gimpel, 2016], and Swishβ(x) = xσ(βx) [Ramachandran et al., 2017].\n\nFFNGELU(x, W1, W2) = GELU(xW1)W2\n\n(3)\n\nFFNSwish(x, W1, W2) = Swish1(xW1)W2", "token_count": 351, "tokenizer": "BAAI/bge-m3", "page_numbers": [1], "source_spans": [{"paragraph_id": "p_291bd1e6572c2b824848", "page": 1, "bbox": [70.8, 347.85, 541.78, 395.13], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 433, "section_char_start": 0, "section_char_end": 433}, {"paragraph_id": "p_1a132f3c196ad27c57b8", "page": 1, "bbox": [71.76, 395.73, 391.57, 405.69], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 73, "section_char_start": 435, "section_char_end": 508}, {"paragraph_id": "p_bbde3ce2bd993f200475", "page": 1, "bbox": [195.48, 419.26, 540.04, 431.01], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 52, "section_char_start": 510, "section_char_end": 562}, {"paragraph_id": "p_864dfe63c9e614d4baa4", "page": 1, "bbox": [87.0, 434.58, 432.98, 447.45], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 81, "section_char_start": 564, "section_char_end": 645}, {"paragraph_id": "p_de3f72ef5f66af045836", "page": 1, "bbox": [219.24, 461.14, 540.04, 471.45], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 38, "section_char_start": 647, "section_char_end": 685}, {"paragraph_id": "p_b3c0762229246e3ae2ec", "page": 1, "bbox": [72.0, 479.37, 540.87, 513.21], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 224, "section_char_start": 687, "section_char_end": 911}, {"paragraph_id": "p_2281e03407afa487c10b", "page": 1, "bbox": [217.32, 534.22, 392.89, 544.53], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 32, "section_char_start": 913, "section_char_end": 945}, {"paragraph_id": "p_09f24cba6c6041640237", "page": 1, "bbox": [527.28, 542.25, 540.04, 552.21], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 3, "section_char_start": 947, "section_char_end": 950}, {"paragraph_id": "p_ab5f0a2820bcf4154c2b", "page": 1, "bbox": [219.84, 549.22, 394.09, 559.53], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 35, "section_char_start": 952, "section_char_end": 987}], "parser_quality_flags": [], "chunker_version": "recursive-spans-v1", "config_id": "2361cccdddd6", "previous_chunk_id": null, "next_chunk_id": null}
{"chunk_id": "chunk_3e5d0dc04a6bca8a6beb30b5", "paper_id": "arxiv_2002.05202", "arxiv_id": "2002.05202", "paper_title": "GLU Variants Improve Transformer", "version": "v1", "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.05202v1", "source_pdf": "data/corpus_80/pdfs/arxiv_2002.05202v1.pdf", "source_pdf_sha256": "7398ae8b163722a6b5410fb975722adbe6b5f6f9c7f1efa584f1c6b79eaa93e2", "section_id": "arxiv_2002.05202_v1_s0003", "section_title": "2 Gated Linear Units (GLU) and Variants", "section_path": ["2 Gated Linear Units (GLU) and Variants"], "section_kind": "body", "section_chunk_index": 0, "char_start": 0, "char_end": 1104, "offset_scope": "normalized_section_text", "text": "[Dauphin et al., 2016] introduced Gated Linear Units (GLU), a neural network layer defined as the component- wise product of two linear transformations of the input, one of which is sigmoid-activated. They also suggest omitting the activation, which they call a \"bilinear\" layer and attribute to [Mnih and Hinton, 2007].\n\nGLU(x, W, V, b, c) = σ(xW + b) ⊗(xV + c)\n\nBilinear(x, W, V, b, c) = (xW + b) ⊗(xV + c) (4)\n\nWe can also define GLU variants using other activation functions:\n\n1Also in the interest of ML fairness.\n\nReGLU(x, W, V, b, c) = max(0, xW + b) ⊗(xV + c)\n\nGEGLU(x, W, V, b, c) = GELU(xW + b) ⊗(xV + c) SwiGLU(x, W, V, b, c, β) = Swishβ(xW + b) ⊗(xV + c)\n\n(5)\n\nIn this paper, we propose additional variations on the Transformer FFN layer which use GLU or one of its variants in place of the first linear transformation and the activation function. Again, we omit the bias terms.\n\nFFNGLU(x, W, V, W2) = (σ(xW) ⊗xV )W2 FFNBilinear(x, W, V, W2) = (xW ⊗xV )W2\n\nFFNReGLU(x, W, V, W2) = (max(0, xW) ⊗xV )W2 FFNGEGLU(x, W, V, W2) = (GELU(xW) ⊗xV )W2 FFNSwiGLU(x, W, V, W2) = (Swish1(xW) ⊗xV )W2\n\n(6)", "token_count": 462, "tokenizer": "BAAI/bge-m3", "page_numbers": [1, 2], "source_spans": [{"paragraph_id": "p_ade51e987f18999e770a", "page": 1, "bbox": [71.64, 601.65, 543.09, 635.61], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 320, "section_char_start": 0, "section_char_end": 320}, {"paragraph_id": "p_1467145f0f00b9dfa997", "page": 1, "bbox": [218.88, 655.6, 405.76, 666.81], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 40, "section_char_start": 322, "section_char_end": 362}, {"paragraph_id": "p_486d05c1665c89923267", "page": 1, "bbox": [206.16, 664.53, 540.04, 681.81], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 48, "section_char_start": 364, "section_char_end": 412}, {"paragraph_id": "p_aeb5a276f42efc09e79a", "page": 1, "bbox": [87.0, 693.21, 374.41, 703.17], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 65, "section_char_start": 414, "section_char_end": 479}, {"paragraph_id": "p_5266da148c5ad6741cdd", "page": 1, "bbox": [83.04, 709.65, 216.77, 720.19], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 37, "section_char_start": 481, "section_char_end": 518}, {"paragraph_id": "p_60c04e7f5cb8cb36d93d", "page": 2, "bbox": [201.72, 92.56, 422.32, 103.77], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 47, "section_char_start": 520, "section_char_end": 567}, {"paragraph_id": "p_3a5164c7a6ab33664c4d", "page": 2, "bbox": [187.32, 107.56, 424.6, 134.17], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 97, "section_char_start": 569, "section_char_end": 666}, {"paragraph_id": "p_b0e7f47873ed6acf0e8e", "page": 2, "bbox": [527.28, 108.93, 540.04, 118.89], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 3, "section_char_start": 668, "section_char_end": 671}, {"paragraph_id": "p_b7a28acb1e00a8a0152b", "page": 2, "bbox": [72.0, 145.17, 540.12, 179.01], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 217, "section_char_start": 673, "section_char_end": 890}, {"paragraph_id": "p_d25be09dc3adb5b13090", "page": 2, "bbox": [197.52, 199.0, 392.53, 225.21], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 75, "section_char_start": 892, "section_char_end": 967}, {"paragraph_id": "p_b3cbbc3acc9872270ed2", "page": 2, "bbox": [195.6, 229.0, 415.81, 270.09], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 130, "section_char_start": 969, "section_char_end": 1099}, {"paragraph_id": "p_0c9dd0e346039733ad6c", "page": 2, "bbox": [527.28, 230.37, 540.04, 240.33], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 3, "section_char_start": 1101, "section_char_end": 1104}], "parser_quality_flags": [], "chunker_version": "recursive-spans-v1", "config_id": "2361cccdddd6", "previous_chunk_id": null, "next_chunk_id": "chunk_dd0e2c8dc36c014cf305d14c"}
{"chunk_id": "chunk_dd0e2c8dc36c014cf305d14c", "paper_id": "arxiv_2002.05202", "arxiv_id": "2002.05202", "paper_title": "GLU Variants Improve Transformer", "version": "v1", "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.05202v1", "source_pdf": "data/corpus_80/pdfs/arxiv_2002.05202v1.pdf", "source_pdf_sha256": "7398ae8b163722a6b5410fb975722adbe6b5f6f9c7f1efa584f1c6b79eaa93e2", "section_id": "arxiv_2002.05202_v1_s0003", "section_title": "2 Gated Linear Units (GLU) and Variants", "section_path": ["2 Gated Linear Units (GLU) and Variants"], "section_kind": "body", "section_chunk_index": 1, "char_start": 1009, "char_end": 1455, "offset_scope": "normalized_section_text", "text": ")W2 FFNGEGLU(x, W, V, W2) = (GELU(xW) ⊗xV )W2 FFNSwiGLU(x, W, V, W2) = (Swish1(xW) ⊗xV )W2\n\n(6)\n\nAll of these layers have three weight matrices, as opposed to two for the original FFN. To keep the number of parameters and the amount of computation constant, we reduce the number of hidden units dff (the second dimension of W and V and the first dimension of W2) by a factor of 2\n\n3 when comparing these layers to the original two-matrix version.", "token_count": 147, "tokenizer": "BAAI/bge-m3", "page_numbers": [2], "source_spans": [{"paragraph_id": "p_b3cbbc3acc9872270ed2", "page": 2, "bbox": [195.6, 229.0, 415.81, 270.09], "paragraph_char_start": 40, "paragraph_char_end": 130, "section_char_start": 1009, "section_char_end": 1099}, {"paragraph_id": "p_0c9dd0e346039733ad6c", "page": 2, "bbox": [527.28, 230.37, 540.04, 240.33], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 3, "section_char_start": 1101, "section_char_end": 1104}, {"paragraph_id": "p_b8d18b3144512f9ebf85", "page": 2, "bbox": [70.8, 281.49, 540.01, 315.33], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 282, "section_char_start": 1106, "section_char_end": 1388}, {"paragraph_id": "p_04c52dba6da0342a6a11", "page": 2, "bbox": [72.0, 305.37, 539.99, 327.33], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 65, "section_char_start": 1390, "section_char_end": 1455}], "parser_quality_flags": [], "chunker_version": "recursive-spans-v1", "config_id": "2361cccdddd6", "previous_chunk_id": "chunk_3e5d0dc04a6bca8a6beb30b5", "next_chunk_id": null}
{"chunk_id": "chunk_78af8a2d85f45e1290e72d06", "paper_id": "arxiv_2002.05202", "arxiv_id": "2002.05202", "paper_title": "GLU Variants Improve Transformer", "version": "v1", "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.05202v1", "source_pdf": "data/corpus_80/pdfs/arxiv_2002.05202v1.pdf", "source_pdf_sha256": "7398ae8b163722a6b5410fb975722adbe6b5f6f9c7f1efa584f1c6b79eaa93e2", "section_id": "arxiv_2002.05202_v1_s0004", "section_title": "3 Experiments on Text-to-Text Transfer Transformer (T5)", "section_path": ["3 Experiments on Text-to-Text Transfer Transformer (T5)"], "section_kind": "body", "section_chunk_index": 0, "char_start": 0, "char_end": 303, "offset_scope": "normalized_section_text", "text": "We test the FFN variants we have described on the transfer-learning setup from [Raffel et al., 2019]. An encoder-decoder transformer model [Vaswani et al., 2017] is trained on a denoising objective of predicting missing text segments, and subsequently fine-tuned on various language understanding tasks.", "token_count": 78, "tokenizer": "BAAI/bge-m3", "page_numbers": [2], "source_spans": [{"paragraph_id": "p_45bdb9c0f64d4009e164", "page": 2, "bbox": [71.52, 372.09, 540.02, 406.05], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 303, "section_char_start": 0, "section_char_end": 303}], "parser_quality_flags": [], "chunker_version": "recursive-spans-v1", "config_id": "2361cccdddd6", "previous_chunk_id": null, "next_chunk_id": null}
{"chunk_id": "chunk_a0ee3672dcfde90a0fc376c5", "paper_id": "arxiv_2002.05202", "arxiv_id": "2002.05202", "paper_title": "GLU Variants Improve Transformer", "version": "v1", "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.05202v1", "source_pdf": "data/corpus_80/pdfs/arxiv_2002.05202v1.pdf", "source_pdf_sha256": "7398ae8b163722a6b5410fb975722adbe6b5f6f9c7f1efa584f1c6b79eaa93e2", "section_id": "arxiv_2002.05202_v1_s0005", "section_title": "3.1 Model Architecture", "section_path": ["3 Experiments on Text-to-Text Transfer Transformer (T5)", "3.1 Model Architecture"], "section_kind": "body", "section_chunk_index": 0, "char_start": 0, "char_end": 946, "offset_scope": "normalized_section_text", "text": "We use the same code base, model architecture, and training task as the base model from [Raffel et al., 2019]. The encoder and decoder each consist of 12 layers, with dmodel = 768. For the attention layers, h = 12 and dk = dv = 64. The FFN layers have hidden size dff = 3072. As we describe above, for the GLU-variant-based FFN layers, which have thee weight matrices instead of two, we reduce the hidden layer to dff = 2048, so as to maintain the same parameter and operation counts as the base model.\n\nTable 1: Heldout-set log-perplexity for Transformer models on the segment-filling task from [Raffel et al., 2019]. All models are matched for parameters and computation.\n\nTraining Steps 65,536 524,288 FFNReLU(baseline) 1.997 (0.005) 1.677 FFNGELU 1.983 (0.005) 1.679 FFNSwish 1.994 (0.003) 1.683 FFNGLU 1.982 (0.006) 1.663 FFNBilinear 1.960 (0.005) 1.648 FFNGEGLU 1.942 (0.004) 1.633 FFNSwiGLU 1.944 (0.010) 1.636 FFNReGLU 1.953 (0.003) 1.645", "token_count": 286, "tokenizer": "BAAI/bge-m3", "page_numbers": [2], "source_spans": [{"paragraph_id": "p_f7c41e17e9aa98bcbd4b", "page": 2, "bbox": [71.52, 442.29, 541.71, 501.57], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 502, "section_char_start": 0, "section_char_end": 502}, {"paragraph_id": "p_4b0be637f69219d82884", "page": 2, "bbox": [71.64, 513.33, 541.33, 535.17], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 169, "section_char_start": 504, "section_char_end": 673}, {"paragraph_id": "p_fc62539c79c6407eee24", "page": 2, "bbox": [205.32, 551.49, 406.99, 657.93], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 271, "section_char_start": 675, "section_char_end": 946}], "parser_quality_flags": [], "chunker_version": "recursive-spans-v1", "config_id": "2361cccdddd6", "previous_chunk_id": null, "next_chunk_id": null}
{"chunk_id": "chunk_a573d3c746884c320e806045", "paper_id": "arxiv_2002.05202", "arxiv_id": "2002.05202", "paper_title": "GLU Variants Improve Transformer", "version": "v1", "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.05202v1", "source_pdf": "data/corpus_80/pdfs/arxiv_2002.05202v1.pdf", "source_pdf_sha256": "7398ae8b163722a6b5410fb975722adbe6b5f6f9c7f1efa584f1c6b79eaa93e2", "section_id": "arxiv_2002.05202_v1_s0006", "section_title": "3.2 Pre-Training and Perplexity Results", "section_path": ["3 Experiments on Text-to-Text Transfer Transformer (T5)", "3.2 Pre-Training and Perplexity Results"], "section_kind": "body", "section_chunk_index": 0, "char_start": 0, "char_end": 1048, "offset_scope": "normalized_section_text", "text": "Identically to [Raffel et al., 2019], we pre-train for 524,288 steps on the span-filling objective on the C4 dataset. Each training batch consists of 128 examples, each of which has an input of 512 tokens and an output of 114 tokens, the output containing multiple spans of tokens which were deleted from the input2. Similarly to [Raffel et al., 2019], we use the Adafactor optimizer [Shazeer and Stern, 2018] and an inverse- square-root learning-rate schedule. We also decay the learning rate linearly for the final 10 percent of the training steps. Our main departure from [Raffel et al., 2019] is that we use no dropout during pre-training. We find this to produce superior results. We compute the log-perplexity on the training objective on a heldout shard of C4, which we believe to be a good indicator of model quality. For each model architecture, we also trained four models for a shorter period (65,536 steps) to measure inter-run variability. The results are listed in table 1. The GEGLU and SwiGLU variants produce the best perplexities.", "token_count": 273, "tokenizer": "BAAI/bge-m3", "page_numbers": [3], "source_spans": [{"paragraph_id": "p_6c98e8e556def6c4ee67", "page": 3, "bbox": [71.52, 92.85, 542.22, 210.45], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 1048, "section_char_start": 0, "section_char_end": 1048}], "parser_quality_flags": [], "chunker_version": "recursive-spans-v1", "config_id": "2361cccdddd6", "previous_chunk_id": null, "next_chunk_id": null}
{"chunk_id": "chunk_ad335ad1637cf256005f2b1a", "paper_id": "arxiv_2002.05202", "arxiv_id": "2002.05202", "paper_title": "GLU Variants Improve Transformer", "version": "v1", "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.05202v1", "source_pdf": "data/corpus_80/pdfs/arxiv_2002.05202v1.pdf", "source_pdf_sha256": "7398ae8b163722a6b5410fb975722adbe6b5f6f9c7f1efa584f1c6b79eaa93e2", "section_id": "arxiv_2002.05202_v1_s0007", "section_title": "3.3 Fine-Tuning", "section_path": ["3 Experiments on Text-to-Text Transfer Transformer (T5)", "3.3 Fine-Tuning"], "section_kind": "body", "section_chunk_index": 0, "char_start": 0, "char_end": 1286, "offset_scope": "normalized_section_text", "text": "We then fine-tune each fully-trained model once on an examples-proportional mixture of the Stanford Question-Answering Dataset (SQuAD) [Rajpurkar et al., 2016] and all the language understanding tasks in the GLUE [Wang et al., 2018] and SuperGlue [Wang et al., 2019] benchmarks.3 Fine-tuning consists of 131072 steps with a learning rate of 10−3. As in training, the input sequences for each step have a combined length of approximately 65,536 tokens. Following [Raffel et al., 2019], we use a dropout rate of 0.1 on the layer outputs, feed-forward hidden-layers and attention weights. The embedding matrices are fixed during fine-tuning.\n\nTables 2, 3 and 4 show results on the development sets. For each task, we report the best score of any of the checkpoints recorded during fine-tuning. While the results are noisy, the new GLU-variants perform best on most of the tasks. For comparison, at the bottom of each of the tables we list the reuslts from [Raffel et al., 2019]. The model is identical to our FFNReLU model. Their results are notably worse, which we believe was caused by their use of dropout during pre-training. Also listed are the inter-run standard deviations measured by [Raffel et al., 2019].\n\nTable 2: GLUE Language-Understanding Benchmark [Wang et al., 2018] (dev).", "token_count": 359, "tokenizer": "BAAI/bge-m3", "page_numbers": [3], "source_spans": [{"paragraph_id": "p_1afc2f88261c5410c117", "page": 3, "bbox": [71.52, 246.81, 540.13, 328.41], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 638, "section_char_start": 0, "section_char_end": 638}, {"paragraph_id": "p_59db6db356be77163506", "page": 3, "bbox": [71.64, 330.45, 540.46, 400.17], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 571, "section_char_start": 640, "section_char_end": 1211}, {"paragraph_id": "p_1725411d1a99e84e745b", "page": 3, "bbox": [132.36, 413.01, 479.3, 422.97], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 73, "section_char_start": 1213, "section_char_end": 1286}], "parser_quality_flags": [], "chunker_version": "recursive-spans-v1", "config_id": "2361cccdddd6", "previous_chunk_id": null, "next_chunk_id": "chunk_9ba37f0f11b6f86001385601"}
