{"chunk_id": "chunk_a54e60a18b8d029478a7c061", "paper_id": "arxiv_2002.05202", "arxiv_id": "2002.05202", "paper_title": "GLU Variants Improve Transformer", "version": "v1", "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.05202v1", "source_pdf": "data/corpus_80/pdfs/arxiv_2002.05202v1.pdf", "source_pdf_sha256": "7398ae8b163722a6b5410fb975722adbe6b5f6f9c7f1efa584f1c6b79eaa93e2", "section_id": "arxiv_2002.05202_v1_s0008", "section_title": "4 Conclusions", "section_path": ["4 Conclusions"], "section_kind": "body", "section_chunk_index": 0, "char_start": 0, "char_end": 810, "offset_scope": "normalized_section_text", "text": "We have extended the GLU family of layers and proposed their use in Transformer. In a transfer-learning setup, the new variants seem to produce better perplexities for the de-noising objective used in pre-training, as well as better results on many downstream language-understanding tasks. These architectures are simple to implement, and have no apparent computational drawbacks. We offer no explanation as to why these architectures seem to work; we attribute their success, as all else, to divine benevolence.\n\n2Each training step took approximately 0.15 seconds on a 32-core TPUv2 cluster. 3This departs from [Raffel et al., 2019], who fine-tuned separately on the different tasks. We chose one fine-tuning run for simplicity.\n\nTable 3: SuperGLUE Language-Understanding Benchmark [Wang et al., 2019] (dev).", "token_count": 205, "tokenizer": "BAAI/bge-m3", "page_numbers": [3, 4], "source_spans": [{"paragraph_id": "p_79742c562085983210c9", "page": 3, "bbox": [71.52, 612.09, 541.61, 669.93], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 512, "section_char_start": 0, "section_char_end": 512}, {"paragraph_id": "p_6cb8f42c622b438d2ca2", "page": 3, "bbox": [72.0, 676.29, 540.15, 705.91], "paragraph_char_start": 0, "paragraph_char_end": 216, "section_char_start": 514, "section_char_end": 730}, {"paragraph_id": "p_760912cd2920ab445eb7", "page": 4, "bbox": [119.76, 